/*
Logic:
If the number is 0 or 1, return it directly because its square root is the number itself.
Since the square root of a positive number lies between 1 and n, initialize the search range with:
low = 1
high = n
Maintain a variable ans to store the best valid square root found so far.
Use Binary Search:
Find the middle value (mid) of the current range.
Compute mid × mid (using long long to avoid overflow).
Compare mid² with n:
If mid² is equal to n, then mid is the exact square root.
If mid² is less than n, then mid is a possible answer. Store it in ans and search the right half to see if a larger valid value exists.
If mid² is greater than n, search the left half because mid is too large.
Continue the binary search until the search range becomes invalid (low > high).
Return ans, which represents the floor (greatest integer less than or equal to) the square root of n.
*/
class Solution {
	public:
	int floorSqrt(int n) {
		// Base cases: sqrt(0) = 0 and sqrt(1) = 1
		if (n == 0 || n == 1)
			return n;
		
		int low = 1, high = n;
		int ans = 1; // Stores the floor value of sqrt(n)
		
		while (low <= high) {
			// Use long long to avoid overflow during multiplication
			long long mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
			
			// mid * mid is calculated as long long, so no overflow
			long long val = mid * mid;
			
			if (val <= n) {
				// mid is a possible answer, search for a larger one
				ans = mid;
				low = mid + 1;
			} else {
				// mid is too large, search in the left half
				high = mid - 1;
			}
		}
		
		return ans;
	}
};
